post-push external verification (F61) -- r130, 2026-08-12
=========================================================

The rule this implements: a push that reports success is not the same as a
reader seeing the new state.  So every URL below was fetched over the public
internet, by the route a reader would use, after the push -- not inspected in
the local tree.

pushed
------
  arithmetic-landscapes   376e42b..267a14c  main -> main   (12 commits)
  mathlab0911.github.io   69119c4..03b15fe  main -> main   (1 commit)


1. the README renders the new state
-----------------------------------
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes/main/README.md
  200, served.  Confirmed in the served bytes:

    - the papers table has exactly three rows, I / II / III, with the
      current titles;
    - the retirement paragraph is present and names p3map_r121 as the mapping
      and the git history as where the files are;
    - "eighteen mechanical checks";
    - the check table ends at C18, with the homepage row;
    - "127 scripts, 198 logs" -- the count C9 verifies;
    - Part II is described as "No hypothesis, and now written out", not as
      owing two steps to the reader.

  One defect found by reading the served bytes rather than the local file:
  the README began with a UTF-8 BOM.  PowerShell 5.1's Set-Content -Encoding
  UTF8 writes one, and I used it for the 197 -> 198 edit an hour earlier.
  GitHub renders it away, so nothing looked wrong anywhere except in the raw
  view a reader gets when they curl it.  Stripped in the follow-up commit.
  This is the second time in this project that PowerShell's BOM has ridden
  into a committed artefact; the first was this round's commit message.


2. a paper serves the new title
-------------------------------
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes/main/paper/paper4.tex
  200, 77 521 characters.  Lines 38-42 of the served file:

      \title{Arithmetic landscapes III:\\
      deformed measures, random sequences, and the coset identity}
      \author{Kentaro Amauchi\thanks{Independent researcher.
      \href{mailto:amkn.sub03@gmail.com}{amkn.sub03@gmail.com},
      \url{https://mathlab0911.github.io/}}}

  The provisional-subtitle note is gone and the author footnote matches
  Parts I and II.  This doubles as the positive control for item 3: the same
  directory, fetched the same way, returns content.


3. the retired files 404
------------------------
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes/main/paper/paper3.tex
  empty -- not on main.

  The control matters here more than the observation: an empty body is what a
  broken fetcher returns too.  Item 2 is that control, and it returned 77 kB
  from the same directory over the same transport in the same minute.


4. the homepage
---------------
  https://mathlab0911.github.io/index.html
  200, and it serves the rewrite: r_A(n) first, the layer definition of Gamma,
  5.3492879..., three parts with current titles and statuses, a Use of AI
  tools section.

  https://mathlab0911.github.io/
  ** served the OLD page ** on the first fetch, roughly one minute after the
  push succeeded and while /index.html was already new: the enumeration
  definition, "order-sensitive invariant", four papers, 5.34920, no
  disclosure.  GitHub Pages had not finished rebuilding, or a cache had not
  expired.

  This is the finding of the round, and it is exactly why this check exists.
  Two green pushes, a green C18 over the local file, and the URL a reader
  actually types was still serving the sentence that C11 has banned since
  r118.  The bare URL is the one in the papers' author footnotes, in the
  README, and in the email that went to two mathematicians.

  Re-fetch of the bare URL is queued for r131; if it has not caught up, the
  cause is a cache and not the push, and that is a different repair.


what this check could not see
-----------------------------
  - Whether the rendered README on github.com looks right; only the raw bytes
    were fetched.  A rendering defect would survive this.
  - The PDFs.  Only the .tex was fetched, and a reader of the release is more
    likely to open the PDF.
  - Whether the mathematics is correct.  Nothing here is about that.
